WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Imagine driving down the highway at like 65 miles an hour.

2
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:05.920
Okay. Sounds pretty standard.

3
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.360
Right. You are seamlessly changing lanes, you're obeying the traffic lights, using your turn

4
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.200
signals, and just perfectly navigating complex interstate exits.

5
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.960
Yeah. Totally running on autopilot.

6
00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:23.360
Exactly. But here's the catch. You have absolutely no idea who you are, where you came from,

7
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:24.240
or where you're going.

8
00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:27.520
Oh, wow. That is terrifying.

9
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:32.080
Right. Your mind has completely wiped your identity, your history, your personal connections.

10
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:34.480
But it kept the procedural stuff online.

11
00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.840
Exactly. It kept your motor skills and your ability to navigate the physical world fully intact.

12
00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.480
I mean, the outward behavior in a scenario like that, it appears entirely functional

13
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.000
and deliberate to anyone watching.

14
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.760
Yeah, nobody driving next to you would even know.

15
00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:53.360
But the internal system, the actual neurological architecture, has basically enacted this

16
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:58.800
radical emergency quarantine of the person's core identity, just to keep the organism moving.

17
00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:01.360
Which brings us to our topic. Welcome to the deep tide.

18
00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.240
Glad to be here.

19
00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:08.000
Our mission today for you listening is to unpack this dense, super complex,

20
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:13.200
but genuinely astonishing set of clinical prep notes regarding dissociative disorders.

21
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:14.720
They are fascinating notes.

22
00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:19.680
They really are. These sources give us a granular look at how the human mind bends,

23
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:25.280
how it actively fragments itself, and how it deploys these radical protective measures

24
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.800
under conditions of extreme inescapable stress.

25
00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.480
And treating these clinical notes merely as a list of diagnostic criteria,

26
00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:37.520
it really misses the broader reality of what we're looking at here.

27
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.720
Yeah, it's not just a checklist.

28
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.640
No, not at all. We are examining a biological map of human survival.

29
00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.080
A survival map. I like that.

30
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:48.800
Because when an environment becomes fundamentally unsurvivable, particularly early in life,

31
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.760
the mind engineers a way to survive the unsurvivable.

32
00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.080
By altering its own foundational operating system.

33
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:54.880
Exactly.

34
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.800
Okay, so let's unpack this. Before we dive into the specific clinical codes,

35
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.920
our sources establish a baseline definition of dissociation.

36
00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.520
Right. The core concept.

37
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:06.320
Yeah. They define it as a disruption in the, quote,

38
00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.480
normally smooth flow of identity, memory, and awareness.

39
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.360
That phrase, the smooth flow, is so crucial.

40
00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:14.320
It really stands out.

41
00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.840
We totally take for granted that the brain acts as this invisible master conductor.

42
00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.960
Synchronizing millions of parallel processes.

43
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.000
Right, into a single unified sense of self.

44
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.560
Like you wake up, you carry the memories of yesterday,

45
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.600
and you experience your environment in real time as a singular cohesive entity.

46
00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.960
And that synchronization requires massive continuous neurological coordination.

47
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:42.880
I mean, the default mode network, the hippocampus, the prefrontal cortex.

48
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:43.920
They're all working overtime.

49
00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.200
They are constantly stitching sensory input and memory together.

50
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:50.640
So dissociation is the active disruption of that stitching process.

51
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:52.800
The brain just stops integrating the data.

52
00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:53.440
Precisely.

53
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.200
So what does this all mean practically?

54
00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.960
The notes provide this overarching anchor for this entire deep dive.

55
00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.720
The reality testing concept.

56
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:04.640
Yes, in all of these dissociative disorders, reality testing stays intact.

57
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.640
Which is such an important distinction.

58
00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.640
Let me test an analogy on you here.

59
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.560
So if intact reality testing is like sitting in a movie theater

60
00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.160
where the film suddenly starts skipping,

61
00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.760
where the audio completely de-syncs from the video.

62
00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:18.800
Okay, I'm picturing it.

63
00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:20.640
It feels incredibly disorienting.

64
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.120
Right.

65
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.040
But you still fundamentally know you are just a person sitting in a theater watching a broken

66
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:27.440
movie.

67
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.640
You haven't lost the awareness of the theater itself.

68
00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:30.880
Right.

69
00:03:30.880 --> 00:03:32.960
That gets us halfway there, actually.

70
00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:37.440
But let's push that analogy further to really understand the clinical distress involved.

71
00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.480
Okay, push it further.

72
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:39.200
How so?

73
00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:44.160
It is not just that you know you are in a theater watching a corrupted film.

74
00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.520
It's that the doors to the theater are locked from the outside.

75
00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:48.160
Oh, man.

76
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.200
That's claustrophobic.

77
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:49.920
Exactly.

78
00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:52.320
You are painfully aware that the movie is glitching.

79
00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.440
You're fully conscious that the experience is distorted.

80
00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.240
But you also know you cannot fix the projector.

81
00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.080
You're just trapped watching the glitch.

82
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:00.800
Right.

83
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.040
The distress and dissociation comes from that exact awareness.

84
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:09.760
You are anchored to base reality enough to know your internal reality is malfunctioning.

85
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:16.960
That locked door element transforms it from just a weird experience into a genuinely terrifying one.

86
00:04:16.960 --> 00:04:17.920
It really does.

87
00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:23.600
And that distinction, the intact reality testing, that is exactly what separates

88
00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:26.640
a dissociative disorder from a psychotic break.

89
00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:27.280
Exactly.

90
00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.960
Because in psychosis, the person doesn't know the movie is broken.

91
00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.560
They believe the movie is reality.

92
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:32.960
Right.

93
00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:36.240
In a psychotic state, the theater basically vanishes.

94
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.760
The person might believe they've been physically transported into the film.

95
00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:44.000
Or that the distorted audio is like a secret message sent directly to them.

96
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:45.120
Precisely.

97
00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:48.080
Reality testing fails completely there.

98
00:04:48.080 --> 00:04:53.280
But with dissociation, the foundational awareness that something is wrong remains online.

99
00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.360
Which is why these patients often present with immense anxiety about their own mental space.

100
00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.680
They know something is off and it's terrifying.

101
00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.040
So we have a foundational understanding of the smooth flow breaking

102
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.000
and the crucial anchor of reality testing staying intact.

103
00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.680
Let's follow the narrative logic of this defense mechanism.

104
00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:11.040
Where does it break first?

105
00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.120
Yeah, what happens when the smooth flow breaks at the absolute highest level,

106
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.560
like the level of identity?

107
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.760
Well, that brings us to dissociative identity disorder,

108
00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.280
which is clinically coded in the notes as F44.81.

109
00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.880
F44.81.

110
00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:25.200
Okay.

111
00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.120
The sources characterize DD by the presence of two or more distinct personality states.

112
00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:34.800
Accompanied by recurrent gaps in memory for everyday events.

113
00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.560
Right, and personal information.

114
00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.920
Since we're looking at this beyond just surface level symptoms,

115
00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.680
let's look at the actual mechanism.

116
00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:42.320
Let's do it.

117
00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:45.760
The notes classify ordinary forgetting as a simple retrieval failure.

118
00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.360
You know, you just misplace your keys.

119
00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:48.400
Standard glitch.

120
00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:49.120
Yeah.

121
00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:51.920
But DD memory gaps are entirely different.

122
00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:56.240
How does the brain actively wall off complete neural pathways,

123
00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.480
like entire compartments of consciousness,

124
00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:00.640
while keeping others perfectly accessible?

125
00:06:00.640 --> 00:06:02.880
We basically have to look at state dependent memory

126
00:06:02.880 --> 00:06:05.280
taken to an absolute structural extreme.

127
00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:06.160
State dependent memory.

128
00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.760
Yeah, so memory encoding is deeply tied to our physiological state.

129
00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:13.280
When a child experiences severe repeated trauma,

130
00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.880
which the sources explicitly highlight as the root cause of DD,

131
00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:21.040
the amygdala triggers an overwhelming flood of stress hormone.

132
00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.480
Just a massive chemical dump.

133
00:06:22.480 --> 00:06:22.960
Right.

134
00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:27.760
The brain learns that to survive this catastrophic hyperarousal,

135
00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:30.480
it must segregate the memories, the skills,

136
00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.440
and the emotional responses associated with the trauma

137
00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:35.760
from the neural networks used for daily functioning.

138
00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:36.880
It builds firewalls.

139
00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:40.160
It creates distinct encapsulated states of consciousness.

140
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:40.640
Yes.

141
00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:41.200
Wow.

142
00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.680
One identity state holds the trauma,

143
00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:46.000
absorbing the unbearable emotional load,

144
00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:49.920
while another identity state remains completely amnesic to that trauma.

145
00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:53.760
So the child can literally still walk into a classroom and learn math.

146
00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:54.400
Exactly.

147
00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:57.440
It is an extraordinary feat of neurological load distribution.

148
00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:00.080
Which makes the treatment protocol outlined in our sources

149
00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:01.680
incredibly challenging, I'd imagine.

150
00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:02.240
Oh, absolutely.

151
00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:06.400
The first line treatment for DD is phase oriented trauma psychotherapy.

152
00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.120
And here's where it gets really interesting for your listeners.

153
00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:10.480
The specific phasing.

154
00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:11.200
Yeah.

155
00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.160
The notes dictate that the very first phase is always safety and stabilization.

156
00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:17.200
Without exception.

157
00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:19.200
But honestly, playing devil's advocate here,

158
00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:21.600
that seems somewhat counterintuitive.

159
00:07:21.600 --> 00:07:22.400
How so?

160
00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.280
Well, if the repressed trauma is the root cause of the entire disorder,

161
00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.320
wouldn't delaying the trauma processing just prolong the patient's suffering?

162
00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.520
Like, why are clinicians instructed to hold off on digging into the root cause?

163
00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.120
What's fascinating here is the biological reality of the nervous system.

164
00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:45.360
The trauma we are discussing isn't a physical wound where you just go in,

165
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.200
extract the bullet, and stitch it up.

166
00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:47.520
Right.

167
00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.200
It's not a surgical extraction.

168
00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:49.680
No.

169
00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:54.400
To the patient's nervous system, the trauma is an ever-present environmental absolute.

170
00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.240
The danger is still right now for them.

171
00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:57.360
Exactly.

172
00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.320
If a clinician bypasses stabilization and immediately tries to unpack the traumatic

173
00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:06.000
memories, they are forcing the brain to access networks.

174
00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:09.440
It's specifically walled off because they were lethal to the system's stability.

175
00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.640
Oh, so you would throw them entirely outside their window of tolerance.

176
00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:16.400
They would enter a state of extreme autonomic hyperarousal.

177
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.240
You wouldn't be healing the trauma at all.

178
00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:20.080
You'd just be re-traumatizing them.

179
00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:20.400
Yes.

180
00:08:20.960 --> 00:08:25.520
You would be proving to the brain that its environment is still fundamentally unsafe.

181
00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:29.280
And what happens when the brain feels that level of overwhelming threat?

182
00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:34.160
Its only programmed response is to deploy its ultimate defense mechanism again.

183
00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.680
You risk forcing the identity to fragment even further.

184
00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:38.400
Wow.

185
00:08:38.400 --> 00:08:40.800
So you could actively make the DID worse.

186
00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:41.680
Precisely.

187
00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.440
Establishing absolute safety and stabilization isn't just prep work.

188
00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.440
It is actively rewiring the nervous system to realize the war is over.

189
00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.400
Which is the prerequisite for any memory integration later.

190
00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:52.880
Right.

191
00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:56.880
You can't process the past if your body thinks you're still fighting for your life in the present.

192
00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.480
Okay, so we see the brain fracture the self to distribute the load.

193
00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:01.680
That's DID.

194
00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:02.320
Right.

195
00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.680
But what happens when the brain decides not to fragment the identity,

196
00:09:05.680 --> 00:09:09.920
but instead just like pulls the plug on the hard drive entirely?

197
00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.440
Ah, that shifts us to the F44.0 clinical code.

198
00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:14.960
Dissociative amnesia.

199
00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:21.440
The notes define dissociative amnesia as the inability to recall important personal information,

200
00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.000
usually surrounding a traumatic or stressful event.

201
00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.840
Right, the data is just inaccessible.

202
00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:30.960
But the most extreme manifestation of this, and arguably one of the most baffling phenomena

203
00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:36.720
in all of clinical psychology, is when this amnesia is accompanied by a fugue.

204
00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.400
The fugue state, yes.

205
00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:42.160
This is where the neurological separation becomes physically kinetic.

206
00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:43.280
Kinetic, right.

207
00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.160
The sources describe the fugue state as purposeful travel or wandering,

208
00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.280
combined with amnesia for one's own identity.

209
00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.960
This brings us back to that highway scenario we opened the deep dive with.

210
00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:55.840
Exactly.

211
00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:59.440
The brain's GPS has completely wiped its destination history.

212
00:09:59.440 --> 00:10:00.800
It deleted the home address.

213
00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:02.400
It erased the driver's profile.

214
00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:06.560
But the engine is running, and the car is navigating traffic flawlessly.

215
00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:09.600
How does the brain manage to keep procedural memory,

216
00:10:09.600 --> 00:10:14.240
like buying a bus ticket, driving a car, ordering food completely online,

217
00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:18.560
while episodic memory, who I am and where I live, goes completely dark?

218
00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:20.560
It comes down to selective retrieval.

219
00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:24.160
What makes that functional separation possible is the brain's ability to

220
00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:29.360
selectively disable episodic retrieval without touching the motor or procedural centers at all.

221
00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:31.360
Because they live in different parts of the brain.

222
00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:32.480
Exactly.

223
00:10:32.480 --> 00:10:38.000
Procedural memory is deeply ingrained in areas like the basal ganglia and the cerebellum.

224
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:38.480
Okay.

225
00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:44.480
Episodic memory, the actual narrative of you, relies heavily on the hippocampus.

226
00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:46.640
Right. The story of my life is hippocampal.

227
00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:47.280
Yes.

228
00:10:47.280 --> 00:10:51.040
So during a fugue state, the massive surge of stress hormones

229
00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:55.920
effectively suppresses hippocampal function, blocking access to biographical data.

230
00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.160
But the basal ganglia just keeps firing away.

231
00:10:58.160 --> 00:10:58.960
It sure does.

232
00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:00.880
Because survival requires mobility.

233
00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:04.080
The brain basically says, I don't know who we are,

234
00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:06.640
but we have to leave this geographic location immediately.

235
00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.160
It is the ultimate flight response.

236
00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:10.320
The ultimate flight response. Wow.

237
00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:14.880
The organism physically removes itself from the environment associated with the unendurable

238
00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:18.400
stress, utilizing all its procedural skills to do so.

239
00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:22.400
While protecting the conscious mind from the trauma by just erasing the self.

240
00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:25.600
It's an incredible, albeit extreme, adaptation.

241
00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:29.760
Looking at the treatment for this, our notes emphasize establishing safety again.

242
00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.840
But they add a highly specific caveat for dissociative amnesia.

243
00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:35.760
The pacing of memory retrieval.

244
00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:41.840
Yeah. The goal of supportive psychotherapy is letting the memory return at a tolerable pace.

245
00:11:41.840 --> 00:11:43.760
This raises an important question, actually.

246
00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:47.200
Right. Because in other trauma or anxiety treatments, like, say,

247
00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:51.120
exposure therapy for severe phobias, collisions sometimes use flooding

248
00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:52.960
to rapidly recalibrate the brain.

249
00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:54.880
They throw you right into the deep end.

250
00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:58.880
Exactly. Why can't you just show a fugue patient their driver's license,

251
00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:02.400
bring their family into the room, and force the system to reboot?

252
00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:06.560
We really have to distinguish between a learned fear and a structural defense here.

253
00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:07.840
Okay. Unpack that.

254
00:12:07.840 --> 00:12:11.040
Flooding a phobia works by triggering a false alarm

255
00:12:11.040 --> 00:12:14.320
and letting the nervous system realize there is no actual danger.

256
00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:15.840
